1
00:00:16,538 --> 00:00:22,880
[SPEAKER_00]: We were speaking this morning about what meditation is.

2
00:00:27,061 --> 00:00:33,363
[SPEAKER_00]: And as I said that to me it was about cleaning the mind.

3
00:00:34,443 --> 00:00:41,005
[SPEAKER_00]: But again, that's not... that needs qualification, you know, describing what is that, you know, what is that all about.

4
00:00:42,065 --> 00:00:45,686
[SPEAKER_00]: And you're saying that it's simply a kind of relaxation.

5
00:00:47,277 --> 00:00:47,617
[SPEAKER_01]: Calming.

6
00:00:48,158 --> 00:00:48,518
[SPEAKER_00]: Calming.

7
00:00:48,778 --> 00:00:49,519
[SPEAKER_01]: Calming yourself.

8
00:00:51,620 --> 00:00:52,361
[SPEAKER_00]: Calming down.

9
00:00:53,062 --> 00:00:53,302
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

10
00:00:54,162 --> 00:00:54,823
[SPEAKER_01]: Calming the mind.

11
00:00:57,465 --> 00:01:08,073
[SPEAKER_01]: Like in broadest... in broadest term, if somebody who doesn't know what meditation is, has no notion of any techniques, anything, if they were to ask, what is... what is meditation?

12
00:01:10,355 --> 00:01:13,517
[SPEAKER_01]: To convey them, you know, without becoming technical or something.

13
00:01:14,278 --> 00:01:16,700
[SPEAKER_01]: Just, oh, well, it's a way of calming your mind.

14
00:01:19,012 --> 00:01:21,194
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when the mind needs calming, you calm it down.

15
00:01:27,840 --> 00:01:36,528
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if I'm agitated, anyone can... most people can get agitated, and you need to calm down, what do you do?

16
00:01:37,088 --> 00:01:44,755
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the whole... that's kind of the whole point, because learning how to calm your mind down skillfully,

17
00:01:45,793 --> 00:01:53,119
[SPEAKER_01]: Without involving sensuality or distraction, that's the wisdom, that's the work, that's the value of it.

18
00:01:54,119 --> 00:02:01,045
[SPEAKER_01]: So, most people would just calm their mind by the means of sensuality, by the means of seeking pleasure.

19
00:02:02,266 --> 00:02:04,087
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what the Buddha said in the suttas.

20
00:02:04,207 --> 00:02:12,734
[SPEAKER_01]: When touched by unpleasant feeling, an untrained mind, the only escape that they know from that pain is sensual pleasure.

21
00:02:14,207 --> 00:02:21,773
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they would seek centrality to pretty much pacify their mind, so the mind calms down in regard to something else that bother them.

22
00:02:23,434 --> 00:02:40,228
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in other words, you're pretty much just replacing, covering up, you know, pasting a cover on the wound that you don't want to deal with, just some nice cooling patch or something, but underneath it's still the same wound.

23
00:02:42,290 --> 00:02:48,151
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the difference between, like, eh... doing meditation for the right reason and doing meditation for the wrong reason.

24
00:02:50,572 --> 00:02:52,953
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, are you doing meditation just to get away from stuff?

25
00:02:53,993 --> 00:02:55,133
[SPEAKER_01]: To not deal with the things?

26
00:02:55,833 --> 00:03:05,536
[SPEAKER_01]: Or to just calm your mind in regard to things that are still there, that are otherwise agitating you?

27
00:03:05,616 --> 00:03:08,856
[SPEAKER_00]: Or am I trying to replace... yeah, replace the pain?

28
00:03:08,876 --> 00:03:09,217
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

29
00:03:09,357 --> 00:03:11,017
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I trying to basically avoid the pain?

30
00:03:13,568 --> 00:03:18,990
[SPEAKER_01]: Or am I trying to calm my mind in regard to the pain, and not try to get rid of it?

31
00:03:19,390 --> 00:03:20,811
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the fundamental difference there.

32
00:03:22,092 --> 00:03:32,777
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why it's important, like, what view a person carries in regard to meditation.

33
00:03:32,797 --> 00:03:37,659
[SPEAKER_01]: See, most of the contemporary meditation notions, even if people have genuine reasons,

34
00:03:38,585 --> 00:03:53,733
[SPEAKER_01]: When you say meditation, it's immediate kind of implication of a special, super pleasant experience that you get if you do a technique, if you're committed to it, this pleasure overwhelms you and you had a very successful meditation.

35
00:03:54,593 --> 00:03:58,155
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's that's not necessarily calming to begin with.

36
00:03:58,715 --> 00:04:02,417
[SPEAKER_01]: And second, it's kind of exactly the same principle as sensuality is.

37
00:04:04,492 --> 00:04:05,614
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I'm bothered by things.

38
00:04:06,196 --> 00:04:15,575
[SPEAKER_01]: Let me make... let me do some work, let me earn, let me gain, so that I can enjoy this vast amount of pleasure that will calm me down and make me very satisfied.

39
00:04:17,823 --> 00:04:22,367
[SPEAKER_01]: So, sometimes people realize that centrality will not necessarily provide that, or cannot.

40
00:04:22,947 --> 00:04:25,329
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, they turn to meditation, but they don't change the attitude.

41
00:04:26,129 --> 00:04:32,814
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there's the whole idea of, like, jhānas being this pleasure that overwhelms you, and it's ecstatic, and you can't even have enough of it, and stuff.

42
00:04:33,435 --> 00:04:39,539
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a complete just... just the replacement of... same views of centrality, just replacing the objects that you usually chase.

43
00:04:40,580 --> 00:04:45,724
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's... that's what's on people's mind, that expectation of, I'm doing this, when will this pleasure overwhelm me?

44
00:04:45,884 --> 00:04:46,624
[SPEAKER_01]: When will it happen?

45
00:04:47,630 --> 00:04:51,991
[SPEAKER_01]: And would you consider yourself calming yourself down if you're cultivating expectations?

46
00:04:53,271 --> 00:04:56,552
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, is expecting things, is that a calming state of mind?

47
00:04:57,512 --> 00:05:00,112
[SPEAKER_01]: Anticipation, is that a calming quality?

48
00:05:00,832 --> 00:05:00,992
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

49
00:05:01,533 --> 00:05:03,413
[SPEAKER_00]: Not at all.

50
00:05:03,453 --> 00:05:09,094
[SPEAKER_01]: And how many people can say they can truly meditate without the slightest view of anticipation?

51
00:05:09,534 --> 00:05:12,535
[SPEAKER_01]: Contemporarily, like when they come across meditation, Buddhist or otherwise.

52
00:05:13,315 --> 00:05:14,415
[SPEAKER_01]: It's implicit.

53
00:05:15,360 --> 00:05:16,040
[SPEAKER_01]: What's happening?

54
00:05:16,561 --> 00:05:21,022
[SPEAKER_01]: Looking what's happening, so that you can interpret what's happening, waiting for things to happen, hoping they will happen.

55
00:05:21,902 --> 00:05:23,723
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, meditating wits and charity.

56
00:05:25,844 --> 00:05:29,505
[SPEAKER_01]: Waiting for things to happen, they will be pleasant, they will solve everything else out.

57
00:05:29,705 --> 00:05:29,845
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

58
00:05:30,605 --> 00:05:33,066
[SPEAKER_01]: You did the suttas, samatha, it's calming the mind.

59
00:05:36,307 --> 00:05:38,448
[SPEAKER_01]: Not getting rid of things that disturb the mind.

60
00:05:39,498 --> 00:05:41,619
[SPEAKER_01]: Getting rid of things that disturb your mind through restraint.

61
00:05:42,099 --> 00:05:55,162
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, when the mind is agitated, on its own account, on account of ignorance, on account of not... having not a rooted greed, aversion, distraction, you can't just get those things out of your mind, because that's what your mind is.

62
00:05:55,202 --> 00:06:01,264
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, you need to learn, as we said before, how to calm yourself down within that endurance.

63
00:06:02,324 --> 00:06:03,224
[SPEAKER_01]: But you will never begin

64
00:06:06,163 --> 00:06:12,107
[SPEAKER_01]: Going in that right direction of calming, for as long as you don't relinquish your views of meditation bringing this pleasure.

65
00:06:13,809 --> 00:06:14,189
[SPEAKER_00]: Meditation...

66
00:06:14,229 --> 00:06:17,331
[SPEAKER_00]: It's almost like an ignoring of what the mind is.

67
00:06:17,351 --> 00:06:17,931
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

68
00:06:18,132 --> 00:06:18,732
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

69
00:06:18,752 --> 00:06:21,754
[SPEAKER_01]: Just trying to get a pleasant experience instead of this unpleasant experience.

70
00:06:21,834 --> 00:06:23,796
[SPEAKER_01]: That's exactly the the Putujana's way.

71
00:06:24,416 --> 00:06:27,758
[SPEAKER_01]: When touched by pain, the only escape he knows is seeking pleasure.

72
00:06:29,440 --> 00:06:35,304
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter whether he seeks pleasure through food, company, others or meditation.

73
00:06:36,370 --> 00:06:39,437
[SPEAKER_01]: He seeks pleasure, because he doesn't want to deal with pain.

74
00:06:41,842 --> 00:06:47,433
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why it's a central view that underlines that meditation, any meditation of that kind.

75
00:06:50,981 --> 00:06:57,526
[SPEAKER_00]: It reminds me that khanti paramaṅga is the highest austerity.

76
00:06:57,726 --> 00:07:00,889
[SPEAKER_00]: Patient endurance is the highest practice.

77
00:07:01,550 --> 00:07:02,330
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the meditation.

78
00:07:02,350 --> 00:07:04,712
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the meditation.

79
00:07:04,732 --> 00:07:07,374
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not about replacing the experience that has arisen.

80
00:07:08,335 --> 00:07:10,317
[SPEAKER_01]: It's about thoroughly knowing it while it's there.

81
00:07:11,018 --> 00:07:17,203
[SPEAKER_01]: But in order to... the point will come, where in order to fully understand it, you need to learn how to stay calm within it.

82
00:07:19,192 --> 00:07:21,233
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the role of the proper samādhi.

83
00:07:21,553 --> 00:07:24,695
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the right view is the necessary basis for the right samādhi.

84
00:07:25,296 --> 00:07:26,657
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the right view will know that.

85
00:07:27,057 --> 00:07:28,718
[SPEAKER_01]: Will know it's about enduring disorder.

86
00:07:29,839 --> 00:07:31,560
[SPEAKER_01]: Mind is disturbed because the mind is not free.

87
00:07:32,420 --> 00:07:36,923
[SPEAKER_01]: But if I just seek to replace it with pleasurable experience, I haven't understood what needs to be understood.

88
00:07:38,024 --> 00:07:42,987
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what if I instead seek to calm it within the disturbing experience, within the agitated mind?

89
00:07:43,067 --> 00:07:44,808
[SPEAKER_01]: I learn how to calm myself within that.

90
00:07:45,832 --> 00:07:51,854
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the agitation disappears, and that with agitation you're still there, and you get to understand it now, because the mind sees clearly when it's calm.

91
00:07:53,514 --> 00:07:55,095
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the pleasure that Buddha talks about.

92
00:07:55,195 --> 00:07:57,616
[SPEAKER_01]: The pleasure that's different than the pleasure of sensuality.

93
00:07:58,136 --> 00:07:59,556
[SPEAKER_01]: Pleasure of not being bothered.

94
00:07:59,576 --> 00:08:00,576
[SPEAKER_01]: Pleasure of being withdrawn.

95
00:08:00,917 --> 00:08:02,017
[SPEAKER_01]: Pleasure of sense restraint.

96
00:08:03,393 --> 00:08:04,774
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a pleasure that you cultivate.

97
00:08:04,894 --> 00:08:07,755
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, pretty much, yeah.

98
00:08:07,955 --> 00:08:14,358
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a pleasure that you cannot crave for more, because it didn't come in that external sense of just a replacement, just something that overwhelms you.

99
00:08:15,038 --> 00:08:19,400
[SPEAKER_01]: So, and even the pleasure of jhāna, it's the pleasure of that withdrawal from beforehand.

100
00:08:19,920 --> 00:08:22,201
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what the pleasure belongs to, not to the jhāna.

101
00:08:23,742 --> 00:08:26,543
[SPEAKER_01]: Having been secluded, he experienced the pleasure of seclusion.

102
00:08:28,498 --> 00:08:38,524
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, his mind becomes confident in that, and then, he basically is abiding in jhāna, as a result of that seclusion and non-activity that we spoke before.

103
00:08:40,025 --> 00:08:41,746
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's truly what is disturbing.

104
00:08:44,107 --> 00:08:45,728
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the movements of the mind.

105
00:08:45,828 --> 00:08:46,589
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't calm it.

106
00:08:48,090 --> 00:08:50,131
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you seeking pleasure is just another movement.

107
00:08:51,605 --> 00:08:54,566
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a fundamental, exactly, another expectation, another anticipation.

108
00:08:54,606 --> 00:08:56,306
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you get it, if you don't get it, it doesn't matter.

109
00:08:56,926 --> 00:09:03,747
[SPEAKER_01]: You are still relying on the perpetual movement of the mind to remain floating, so to speak.

110
00:09:04,007 --> 00:09:05,207
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's not peaceful.

111
00:09:06,648 --> 00:09:07,768
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not calming.

112
00:09:07,828 --> 00:09:09,608
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you get all the pleasure you want in the world.

113
00:09:12,589 --> 00:09:19,450
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the true Samatha, then, is calming of those movements, of those activities, those saṅkharas.

114
00:09:20,867 --> 00:09:23,269
[SPEAKER_01]: Intentions of the mind.

115
00:09:23,429 --> 00:09:28,093
[SPEAKER_01]: Calming that within whatever you're intending towards or against.

116
00:09:29,234 --> 00:09:37,681
[SPEAKER_01]: But in order to calm your intentions on the mental level, you need to have calmed your body, you need to have calmed your speech, you need to have restrained it sufficiently long before.

117
00:09:38,321 --> 00:09:41,244
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where jhāna begins with you keeping the virtue, pretty much.

118
00:09:42,365 --> 00:09:43,566
[SPEAKER_01]: That's already calming.

119
00:09:44,306 --> 00:09:46,268
[SPEAKER_01]: Stealing of the activities, as the Buddha would say.

120
00:09:46,328 --> 00:09:47,469
[SPEAKER_01]: Stealing of the sankhāras.

121
00:09:47,489 --> 00:09:48,670
[SPEAKER_01]: There's nothing magical about it.

122
00:09:50,817 --> 00:09:52,257
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just there is intention to move.

123
00:09:52,497 --> 00:09:56,478
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't necessarily move through that intention.

124
00:09:56,759 --> 00:09:57,779
[SPEAKER_01]: You see where it's rooted.

125
00:09:58,279 --> 00:10:00,039
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it intention rooted in agitation?

126
00:10:01,320 --> 00:10:02,040
[SPEAKER_01]: Then you endure it.

127
00:10:02,560 --> 00:10:07,521
[SPEAKER_01]: Then you try to not get rid of the agitation, but remove intention to move in the face of agitation.

128
00:10:08,842 --> 00:10:10,262
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's quite subtle already.

129
00:10:10,960 --> 00:10:20,984
[SPEAKER_01]: But if somebody has been thoroughly withdrawn, physically, verbally, not delighting in company, living in seclusion, yeah, that would be a very visible thing.

130
00:10:22,185 --> 00:10:23,185
[SPEAKER_01]: You see why you suffer.

131
00:10:23,245 --> 00:10:31,889
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because there is the disturbing thought, it's because the mind feels confined by it and wants to get rid of it.

132
00:10:31,929 --> 00:10:33,410
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the root of suffering.

133
00:10:35,491 --> 00:10:38,732
[SPEAKER_01]: That movement of that craving, not what has arisen.

134
00:10:40,879 --> 00:10:48,063
[SPEAKER_01]: But you need to have been thoroughly restrained and withdrawn, and then you can actually even start calming that movement of the mind.

135
00:10:49,684 --> 00:10:53,346
[SPEAKER_00]: Not at the expense of what is agitating you.

136
00:10:54,646 --> 00:10:55,206
[SPEAKER_01]: That remains.

137
00:10:55,367 --> 00:10:59,109
[SPEAKER_01]: That actually serves almost as a criteria, whether you're calm now or not.

138
00:11:00,049 --> 00:11:02,130
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's easy to be calm when there's nothing bothering you.

139
00:11:03,331 --> 00:11:07,133
[SPEAKER_01]: But why do people don't want to live in seclusion?

140
00:11:08,418 --> 00:11:15,441
[SPEAKER_01]: Avoid... not partaking company, not practice sense restraining, guard the sense doors, because it's very disturbing.

141
00:11:16,682 --> 00:11:23,085
[SPEAKER_01]: You start saying no to sensuality, guarding the sense doors, avoiding company, living alone, it's very disturbing for the mind.

142
00:11:25,806 --> 00:11:26,806
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's used to it.

143
00:11:27,026 --> 00:11:29,107
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, exactly, because it depends on the movements, yeah?

144
00:11:29,868 --> 00:11:34,850
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the... the life is defined by the movements, by activities, by engagements, by intentions.

145
00:11:34,930 --> 00:11:36,971
[SPEAKER_00]: And when you sit still, so to speak,

146
00:11:38,790 --> 00:11:41,052
[SPEAKER_00]: Mentally, you know, you try to calm down.

147
00:11:41,092 --> 00:11:41,292
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

148
00:11:41,352 --> 00:11:42,053
[SPEAKER_00]: You sit still.

149
00:11:42,413 --> 00:11:42,953
[SPEAKER_00]: You sit still.

150
00:11:43,053 --> 00:11:51,921
[SPEAKER_00]: You then are faced with that whole flow of where you... the mind's... the direction that mind's going.

151
00:11:51,941 --> 00:11:52,641
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, sure, sure.

152
00:11:52,721 --> 00:11:55,824
[SPEAKER_00]: You feel the pressure of its direction.

153
00:11:55,844 --> 00:12:03,470
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to sit still, you want to feel the pressure, but then you don't want to distract yourself from that mental side of it.

154
00:12:04,030 --> 00:12:07,293
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then you realize, yeah, you can walk up and down, you can move around,

155
00:12:08,123 --> 00:12:12,667
[SPEAKER_01]: But, none of the bodily actions should be rooted in that agitation that you want to get rid of.

156
00:12:14,428 --> 00:12:22,435
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you're just pacing up and down, it's not, it's not really the activity that's connected to the activity of the mind that wants to avoid things that are agitating it.

157
00:12:24,076 --> 00:12:26,819
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, that's why the gradual training, it's about building that up.

158
00:12:27,719 --> 00:12:29,981
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, that is the most disturbing thing.

159
00:12:31,683 --> 00:12:35,186
[SPEAKER_01]: Solitude, seclusion, withdrawal from the senses, confinement,

160
00:12:36,182 --> 00:12:37,922
[SPEAKER_01]: It's... it basically feels like dying.

161
00:12:38,403 --> 00:12:40,443
[SPEAKER_01]: Feels like withdrawal of a heavy addict.

162
00:12:41,523 --> 00:12:46,064
[SPEAKER_01]: Addict who's been depending on the senses for years, not just in this life.

163
00:12:47,645 --> 00:12:49,285
[SPEAKER_01]: So... and that's why it's not optional, really.

164
00:12:49,605 --> 00:12:59,987
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just more like whether you... you're gonna take it seriously and work towards it, or you're gonna develop the view that will kind of convince you that you're exempted from it.

165
00:13:00,307 --> 00:13:02,708
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, you know, you can just sit in your room for half an hour,

166
00:13:04,269 --> 00:13:08,251
[SPEAKER_01]: You go into some state of concentration and think you're somehow doing the work by being unaware.

167
00:13:10,732 --> 00:13:13,133
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why gradual training naturally results in jhāna.

168
00:13:16,855 --> 00:13:29,020
[SPEAKER_01]: Restrained by the virtue, moderate in eating, watchful, wakeful, guarding the sense doors, mind's withdrawn and withdrawn and withdrawn, seclusion, and then he enters and lives in the first jhāna.

169
00:13:31,424 --> 00:13:36,109
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is basically learning how to live apart from the dependence on the pleasure of the senses.

170
00:13:36,129 --> 00:13:38,511
[SPEAKER_01]: And for that, you need to have been withdrawn sufficiently.

171
00:13:39,732 --> 00:13:49,342
[SPEAKER_00]: Because an addict who's trying to give up, whatever, cigarettes, then you go through these withdrawal symptoms.

172
00:13:50,182 --> 00:13:53,826
[SPEAKER_00]: But, you know, difficult, whatever, week, a month, whatever it is.

173
00:13:55,994 --> 00:13:57,235
[SPEAKER_00]: You're agitated and so on.

174
00:13:58,235 --> 00:14:00,416
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you're okay.

175
00:14:01,237 --> 00:14:06,099
[SPEAKER_00]: But, sometimes, it gets... people try to replace that with something else.

176
00:14:06,139 --> 00:14:06,519
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure.

177
00:14:06,539 --> 00:14:08,000
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, because it's a relative addiction.

178
00:14:08,220 --> 00:14:11,722
[SPEAKER_01]: But we're not talking about one particular thing that you're addicted through your senses.

179
00:14:12,502 --> 00:14:13,343
[SPEAKER_01]: There are other whole senses.

180
00:14:13,563 --> 00:14:14,123
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, so...

181
00:14:14,143 --> 00:14:22,567
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you think cigarettes are hard to give up a heroin, well, you'll see if you try giving up the actual pleasure dependent on the senses and existence in general.

182
00:14:23,087 --> 00:14:24,048
[SPEAKER_01]: But that is the way out.

183
00:14:24,668 --> 00:14:25,469
[SPEAKER_01]: That is the way out.

184
00:14:25,529 --> 00:14:26,610
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why it is the way out.

185
00:14:26,851 --> 00:14:30,755
[SPEAKER_00]: But you... on just on that small little... You get a glimpse, sure.

186
00:14:31,255 --> 00:14:32,877
[SPEAKER_00]: A glimpse of...

187
00:14:33,037 --> 00:14:34,719
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm now not agitated by...

188
00:14:34,739 --> 00:14:35,360
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

189
00:14:35,600 --> 00:14:38,624
[SPEAKER_00]: ...by being withdrawn from this...

190
00:14:38,704 --> 00:14:44,210
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can imagine then the pleasure even of the first jhāna, when you're not drawn by anything in the world.

191
00:14:45,702 --> 00:14:48,244
[SPEAKER_00]: You're no longer addicted by the sense functions.

192
00:14:48,424 --> 00:14:49,225
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, no addiction.

193
00:14:49,385 --> 00:14:51,747
[SPEAKER_01]: You are completely dry of it.

194
00:14:51,867 --> 00:14:57,151
[SPEAKER_01]: And even if you have the thoughts, they are not central thoughts, like before, you know, the ones that would immediately pressure you.

195
00:14:57,951 --> 00:15:00,513
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's a true release, laying down of the burden.

196
00:15:02,575 --> 00:15:03,896
[SPEAKER_01]: But there is no magical way.

197
00:15:04,676 --> 00:15:08,779
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, watch your left nostril, watch your right, three steps left, turn around, do this, do that.

198
00:15:08,859 --> 00:15:09,039
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

199
00:15:09,380 --> 00:15:13,523
[SPEAKER_01]: The withdrawal from that which you're addicted to, that is the magical way.

200
00:15:15,675 --> 00:15:17,856
[SPEAKER_01]: And you need to be withdrawn sufficiently.

201
00:15:17,916 --> 00:15:18,296
[SPEAKER_01]: How long?

202
00:15:18,336 --> 00:15:20,077
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that depends how strong your addiction was.

203
00:15:21,838 --> 00:15:22,639
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, every time.

204
00:15:23,800 --> 00:15:26,341
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the meditation that the Lord praises, the Buddha praises?

205
00:15:26,901 --> 00:15:28,042
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's this meditation.

206
00:15:28,582 --> 00:15:30,963
[SPEAKER_01]: Here, I say, come, Bhikkhu, with well-restraint, with patimokkha.

207
00:15:31,524 --> 00:15:32,464
[SPEAKER_01]: Be moderate in eating.

208
00:15:32,965 --> 00:15:33,545
[SPEAKER_01]: Be watchful.

209
00:15:33,705 --> 00:15:34,926
[SPEAKER_01]: God just sends those, Bhikkhu.

210
00:15:37,607 --> 00:15:38,408
[SPEAKER_01]: Live in seclusion.

211
00:15:40,109 --> 00:15:42,310
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't... don't... don't engage in company.

212
00:15:44,017 --> 00:15:47,942
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

213
00:15:48,142 --> 00:15:48,903
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the technique.

214
00:15:50,105 --> 00:15:53,469
[SPEAKER_01]: Technique of basically overcoming the addiction of the addictions.

215
00:15:53,749 --> 00:15:54,770
[SPEAKER_01]: The addiction of the senses.

216
00:15:57,433 --> 00:15:58,415
[SPEAKER_01]: Without replacements.

217
00:15:58,675 --> 00:16:01,038
[SPEAKER_01]: And by the way, yeah, that's how people can replace it.

218
00:16:02,002 --> 00:16:13,566
[SPEAKER_01]: Through strong determination, committing to something else, committing to even more activity, constant work, perpetual distraction, company, can keep you, like, your mind away from seeking core sensuality.

219
00:16:13,666 --> 00:16:14,986
[SPEAKER_01]: But you're not uprooting anything.

220
00:16:15,487 --> 00:16:19,408
[SPEAKER_01]: That's just like a herring addict finding something else to keep his mind addicted to.

221
00:16:19,428 --> 00:16:20,708
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not dealing with it.

222
00:16:20,748 --> 00:16:21,949
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not free from addiction.

223
00:16:22,029 --> 00:16:22,449
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, no.

224
00:16:22,469 --> 00:16:25,670
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the moment those activities become unavailable,

225
00:16:27,048 --> 00:16:30,290
[SPEAKER_01]: His resting spot of his mind is where his addiction still is.

226
00:16:30,630 --> 00:16:33,552
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he'll go back to that which he hasn't uprooted.

227
00:16:35,053 --> 00:16:36,214
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what meditation is.

228
00:16:37,274 --> 00:16:49,262
[SPEAKER_01]: Once you have basically, sufficiently, wholesomely disturbed your mind by unwavering sense restraint and guarding of the sense doors, seclusion, now you learn how to calm within that.

229
00:16:50,162 --> 00:16:52,624
[SPEAKER_01]: Not abandon it, but calm.

230
00:16:55,119 --> 00:17:02,642
[SPEAKER_01]: It's still the agitation aspect of it, so that you can carry on, because you're realizing already that that's the way out.

231
00:17:03,123 --> 00:17:06,484
[SPEAKER_00]: But how do you still be the agitated aspect?

232
00:17:06,704 --> 00:17:11,366
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, if I tell you how to do it, there will be another activity you're gonna do.

233
00:17:12,287 --> 00:17:22,932
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only way to do it is to endure it, and through that endurance, when you have no room to move left, right, forward or backward, you will find a way out to calm yourself down.

234
00:17:23,773 --> 00:17:24,493
[SPEAKER_01]: And how would that be?

235
00:17:24,693 --> 00:17:27,394
[SPEAKER_01]: By not moving left, right, forward or backward.

236
00:17:27,654 --> 00:17:28,494
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what you should do.

237
00:17:32,555 --> 00:17:34,416
[SPEAKER_01]: How did Master Gautama cross the shore then?

238
00:17:35,096 --> 00:17:38,317
[SPEAKER_01]: By not fighting the waves, by not pulling backward, by not standing still.

239
00:17:38,417 --> 00:17:39,237
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I did.

240
00:17:43,278 --> 00:17:44,638
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you're pushing forward, don't.

241
00:17:45,238 --> 00:17:46,058
[SPEAKER_01]: Pulling backward, don't.

242
00:17:46,078 --> 00:17:46,839
[SPEAKER_01]: Standing still, don't.

243
00:17:48,919 --> 00:17:52,160
[SPEAKER_01]: But those, eh...

244
00:17:53,162 --> 00:17:59,009
[SPEAKER_01]: Activities, movements, would become apparent if you have been sufficiently restrained and lived withdrawn.

245
00:18:04,036 --> 00:18:05,357
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's also why often in this...

246
00:18:05,418 --> 00:18:06,859
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, I said that many times, probably, but...

247
00:18:08,221 --> 00:18:10,524
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not... it's not... you can't accidentally become enlightened.

248
00:18:10,957 --> 00:18:19,599
[SPEAKER_01]: Who will accidentally become free from addiction through enduring the pain of withdrawal for six months, without a reason, without a determined goal to go through that?

249
00:18:20,539 --> 00:18:26,580
[SPEAKER_01]: Who would accidentally just choose to do that perpetually for so long that they actually become free?

250
00:18:26,700 --> 00:18:26,840
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

251
00:18:26,880 --> 00:18:35,161
[SPEAKER_01]: There has to be some knowledge, some will behind to commit to that training, to see some benefit in it.

252
00:18:35,621 --> 00:18:36,681
[SPEAKER_00]: Peace, nibbāna.

253
00:18:37,202 --> 00:18:37,582
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

254
00:18:37,602 --> 00:18:39,462
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe just that idea of ultimate

255
00:18:45,591 --> 00:18:54,745
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why people, when they did, you know, people they were not restrained or interested in practice, they encountered the Buddha when and they insisted to know what he's teaching.

256
00:18:55,688 --> 00:19:01,112
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, he would describe the nibbāna to them, they would be shocked and say, well, that's death, that will kill me.

257
00:19:01,132 --> 00:19:02,833
[SPEAKER_00]: Relinquishment, yeah, relinquishment of what I am.

258
00:19:03,053 --> 00:19:10,578
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that will kill me, that will destroy what I am, because it will, because whatever people are, is what is based on the senses and sense pleasures.

259
00:19:10,598 --> 00:19:12,279
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, as you said in the past, why...

260
00:19:13,560 --> 00:19:15,682
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it difficult to give up cigarettes?

261
00:19:15,762 --> 00:19:16,983
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that is...

262
00:19:17,003 --> 00:19:17,563
[SPEAKER_00]: Your whole world.

263
00:19:17,743 --> 00:19:18,224
[SPEAKER_00]: Your world.

264
00:19:19,224 --> 00:19:20,025
[SPEAKER_00]: That you are.

265
00:19:20,265 --> 00:19:20,605
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

266
00:19:20,785 --> 00:19:21,726
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it would be like dying.

267
00:19:21,766 --> 00:19:27,590
[SPEAKER_01]: And again, cigarettes, heroin, alcohol, all of those are particular addictions within sensuality.

268
00:19:28,031 --> 00:19:32,514
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you're giving up, basically, pleasure of the senses.

269
00:19:32,534 --> 00:19:35,616
[SPEAKER_01]: Pleasure of the activity through the senses.

270
00:19:36,197 --> 00:19:39,860
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only way to do that successfully is gradual training.

271
00:19:40,747 --> 00:19:52,311
[SPEAKER_01]: You have a wide base of unshakable virtue, wide base of sense restraint, moderation in eating, watchfulness, wide base of guarding the sense doors.

272
00:19:53,632 --> 00:20:01,515
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, like, you know, all the struggle that your mind will go through for withdrawal, you can come within that without sort of falling off that base.

273
00:20:08,067 --> 00:20:10,028
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a boundary, then, that you can... Yeah.

274
00:20:10,329 --> 00:20:22,257
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why I said, like, you know, it's it's actually from the point of view of developing your mind, it's better, because people sometimes ask, oh, well, you know, okay, I understand I want to meditate, but I understand I have to keep the precepts in order to meditate.

275
00:20:22,978 --> 00:20:25,520
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, first, well, why do you want to meditate, specifically?

276
00:20:26,480 --> 00:20:28,281
[SPEAKER_01]: You're saying, yes, meditation itself is a goal.

277
00:20:29,723 --> 00:20:30,823
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you're still seeking pleasure.

278
00:20:31,784 --> 00:20:33,685
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, second, okay, so you want to keep the precepts.

279
00:20:33,705 --> 00:20:34,866
[SPEAKER_01]: So, do I keep five or eight, or

280
00:20:37,150 --> 00:20:42,993
[SPEAKER_01]: It's actually better if... oh, shall I keep eight precepts, you know, once a week or something?

281
00:20:44,594 --> 00:20:49,316
[SPEAKER_01]: It's better to keep the five precepts, but once you take them, you take them for the rest of your life.

282
00:20:50,637 --> 00:20:52,057
[SPEAKER_01]: Not for two days, five days.

283
00:20:53,678 --> 00:20:55,899
[SPEAKER_01]: That becomes that wide base I'm talking about.

284
00:20:56,359 --> 00:20:57,540
[SPEAKER_00]: It has to be done in that sense.

285
00:20:58,681 --> 00:20:58,901
[SPEAKER_01]: You know.

286
00:20:59,321 --> 00:21:02,302
[SPEAKER_01]: A herineric will not free himself if he goes to rehab only on the weekends.

287
00:21:03,883 --> 00:21:04,924
[SPEAKER_01]: And then he's back to the world.

288
00:21:05,722 --> 00:21:06,983
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just silly.

289
00:21:08,644 --> 00:21:13,066
[SPEAKER_01]: So, take on the five precepts forever.

290
00:21:14,987 --> 00:21:19,429
[SPEAKER_01]: That already is going to be confinement for a mind of addiction of sensuality.

291
00:21:20,150 --> 00:21:21,710
[SPEAKER_01]: That's already... oh, okay.

292
00:21:21,951 --> 00:21:25,292
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the weight you need to start taking on in the gradual training.

293
00:21:25,352 --> 00:21:26,173
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the whole point.

294
00:21:27,718 --> 00:21:44,647
[SPEAKER_01]: Like even, even when, when, when, you know, ārūpa-sampada, when they, when, when, when the chanters recite, when they recite the four parājikas, four grave offenses, if you commit, you're not a monk, and then it says what those things are, it's not to be done for the rest of your life by you.

295
00:21:45,508 --> 00:21:49,370
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just not like, it's, it has to be taken in the ultimate sense.

296
00:21:49,810 --> 00:21:54,433
[SPEAKER_01]: So it's better to even take, like, just five precepts, but in an ultimate sense.

297
00:21:54,493 --> 00:21:56,774
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what all the lay followers in the suttas did.

298
00:21:58,000 --> 00:22:00,001
[SPEAKER_01]: I go to the refuge for the rest of my life.

299
00:22:00,381 --> 00:22:02,342
[SPEAKER_01]: Let Buddha know me as a lay follower.

300
00:22:04,222 --> 00:22:14,226
[SPEAKER_01]: Means, this now is the basis of my death-sensory-strain confinement, the five precepts, which is very easy.

301
00:22:17,955 --> 00:22:19,516
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not even confining, confining.

302
00:22:19,576 --> 00:22:19,796
[SPEAKER_01]: So...

303
00:22:20,557 --> 00:22:31,764
[SPEAKER_01]: But, as I said, take that in the ultimate sense, and then, on top of that, if sometimes you want to keep the Eight Precepts, yes, but when you don't keep the Eight Precepts, there is no falling lower than the Five Precepts.

304
00:22:32,004 --> 00:22:32,905
[SPEAKER_01]: That is your basis.

305
00:22:33,185 --> 00:22:34,466
[SPEAKER_01]: That must remain your basis.

306
00:22:35,447 --> 00:22:42,911
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, if you really feel, no, no, no, I do really want to free myself once and for all, or at least make an attempt for it, then you take the Eight Precepts.

307
00:22:43,892 --> 00:22:45,133
[SPEAKER_01]: For the rest of your life.

308
00:22:46,470 --> 00:22:50,293
[SPEAKER_01]: You take up that basis in the ultimate sense of eight precepts.

309
00:22:51,093 --> 00:22:51,494
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

310
00:22:51,634 --> 00:22:54,576
[SPEAKER_01]: Through that, because now that's something you must protect.

311
00:22:55,717 --> 00:22:57,498
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, you're forced to be mindful.

312
00:22:58,679 --> 00:23:01,061
[SPEAKER_01]: You're forced to be moderate and not overdo things.

313
00:23:01,101 --> 00:23:03,342
[SPEAKER_01]: They will make you spill out and break the precepts.

314
00:23:04,243 --> 00:23:13,850
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, you are forced to watch your senses, garden, because if you don't, it becomes very unpleasant within the confinement of the eight precepts and the mind that wants to do that, which is outside of the eight precepts.

315
00:23:16,109 --> 00:23:17,570
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you start taming it.

316
00:23:19,291 --> 00:23:37,624
[SPEAKER_01]: In all the similes in the suttas, you know, get a wild elephant from the forest, from the jungle, where he was just roaming around, strap it down with big, you know, peg and leather straps, and then wait for it to calm down, because it's just gonna... when it comes down, then...

317
00:23:39,068 --> 00:23:46,350
[SPEAKER_01]: Loosen up a bit, move it left, move it right, poke it, train it, feed it, until it starts to accept it.

318
00:23:47,110 --> 00:23:48,751
[SPEAKER_01]: And, yeah, that's the mind.

319
00:23:51,211 --> 00:23:52,552
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not about do what you feel like.

320
00:23:52,672 --> 00:23:52,952
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

321
00:23:53,012 --> 00:23:56,633
[SPEAKER_01]: You are going against the entire grain of saṃsāra, of existence.

322
00:23:57,713 --> 00:24:00,814
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not just seeking one pleasant experience to replace the other one.

323
00:24:01,114 --> 00:24:02,475
[SPEAKER_01]: You're uprooting all of it.

324
00:24:03,455 --> 00:24:04,955
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's meditation.

325
00:24:05,656 --> 00:24:06,796
[SPEAKER_01]: Calming amidst that.

326
00:24:09,201 --> 00:24:10,782
[SPEAKER_00]: Withdrawal.

327
00:24:11,342 --> 00:24:14,903
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, withdrawal, the wild mind still kicks and screams.

328
00:24:15,483 --> 00:24:26,926
[SPEAKER_01]: You're calming it without abandoning withdrawal, without giving in to the senses, without letting the elephant go back to the to the jungle, to appease itself, to have a break.

329
00:24:26,986 --> 00:24:27,167
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

330
00:24:27,327 --> 00:24:27,627
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no.

331
00:24:28,327 --> 00:24:29,707
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no going back.

332
00:24:31,088 --> 00:24:31,768
[SPEAKER_01]: Non-returner.

333
00:24:34,489 --> 00:24:34,829
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

334
00:24:34,889 --> 00:24:37,250
[SPEAKER_01]: One who abandons sensuality is not coming back to this world.

335
00:24:37,630 --> 00:24:38,110
[SPEAKER_01]: Impossible.

336
00:24:39,063 --> 00:24:46,189
[SPEAKER_01]: For that very reason, he's abandoned dependence on anything from this world.

337
00:24:46,209 --> 00:24:48,531
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not a poetic name, non-returning.

338
00:24:49,511 --> 00:24:49,972
[SPEAKER_01]: Factual.

339
00:24:53,935 --> 00:24:57,518
[SPEAKER_00]: He's never returning to any of the previous addictions.

340
00:24:57,578 --> 00:24:58,679
[SPEAKER_01]: He won't be returning, no.

341
00:24:59,019 --> 00:24:59,439
[SPEAKER_01]: He cannot.

342
00:25:00,040 --> 00:25:00,760
[SPEAKER_01]: He's been abandoned.

343
00:25:00,900 --> 00:25:01,741
[SPEAKER_00]: No matter what happens.

344
00:25:02,141 --> 00:25:02,762
[SPEAKER_01]: No matter what happens.

345
00:25:04,235 --> 00:25:04,916
[SPEAKER_01]: It's inconceivable.

346
00:25:05,636 --> 00:25:08,278
[SPEAKER_01]: Like a stone split in half, cannot be joined together.

347
00:25:09,919 --> 00:25:11,801
[SPEAKER_01]: As a dry leaf cannot become green again.

348
00:25:12,521 --> 00:25:14,242
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, just irreversible.

349
00:25:15,904 --> 00:25:19,887
[SPEAKER_01]: If you accomplish the grain against the saṃsāra, it's irreversible.

350
00:25:20,007 --> 00:25:21,088
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot go back down.

351
00:25:21,728 --> 00:25:22,168
[SPEAKER_01]: Impossible.

352
00:25:22,208 --> 00:25:24,130
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why free mind is known as... Oh, it's free.

353
00:25:31,232 --> 00:25:39,214
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like... an addict, an alcoholic, would be like... not drinking for 10 years, but can they still go back?

354
00:25:40,355 --> 00:25:42,715
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah, because they haven't uprooted their sensuality.

355
00:25:43,015 --> 00:25:44,536
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, they're not non-returners.

356
00:25:44,956 --> 00:25:45,096
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

357
00:25:45,136 --> 00:25:46,876
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

358
00:25:46,896 --> 00:25:51,958
[SPEAKER_01]: But in a way, yeah, like, many of them would not... even...

359
00:25:51,998 --> 00:25:59,840
[SPEAKER_01]: I think even in that sense, you can get a glimpse, because once you... like with cigarettes, for example, you free yourself from that addiction, you go back to cigarettes, and it's sickening.

360
00:26:00,260 --> 00:26:00,440
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

361
00:26:01,613 --> 00:26:06,880
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it will take work now, to work through, to become addicted again.

362
00:26:07,060 --> 00:26:12,888
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's kind of impossible for you to go through that work carelessly, knowing what you've been through.

363
00:26:14,067 --> 00:26:20,408
[SPEAKER_01]: When you truly, freely, freed yourself from the addiction of it... And there's nothing that actually offers you... No.

364
00:26:20,528 --> 00:26:20,709
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

365
00:26:20,909 --> 00:26:21,069
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

366
00:26:21,329 --> 00:26:21,629
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

367
00:26:21,669 --> 00:26:27,630
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just simple carelessness and dependence on pleasure, unchecked dependence on pleasure, that people become addicted to stuff.

368
00:26:27,670 --> 00:26:35,812
[SPEAKER_01]: So, by having fully approved the sensuality, fully understood the extent of the danger of it, it's just impossible for mind to even entertain that as a possibility anymore.

369
00:26:37,608 --> 00:26:43,052
[SPEAKER_01]: Literally, that elephant you dragged out of the forest to tame it has finally understood, seen for himself.

370
00:26:43,932 --> 00:26:45,013
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to tell it anymore.

371
00:26:46,074 --> 00:26:47,535
[SPEAKER_01]: The peril of going back there.

372
00:26:48,415 --> 00:26:50,557
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you can, you can unhook it.

373
00:26:50,977 --> 00:26:52,338
[SPEAKER_00]: You can remove all the straps.

374
00:26:52,458 --> 00:26:53,519
[SPEAKER_00]: It will not go back there.

375
